're seeing  in the image here  and again as you can see from this image  on the left the extent of coverage  observed uh  with with both uh cy5 and  with the brightfield signal match up  quite well showing  that the mmp14  proteinase is active in  pretty much every area of the invading  tumeric structure  then if we look closer at the images  on the right shown here highlighted with  the yellow areas and then shown here  as well as as multiple areas with this  picture we can also see that the signal  increases  in the invadopodia meaning that mp14  is indeed quite active in these  leading portions of the invading tumeric  structure  uh we can also perform uh cellular  analysis  using the sci-fi signal from the die and  uh as you can see here and with both the  uh the images and the graphs that i'm  showing on this slide  the object masks are drawn in a manner  similar to what i  explained previously so again you're not  using gfp  or right field signal but actually using  specifically the sci-fi  signal to have gen 5 draw the object  masks  around the around the  parts of the tumorite structure meeting  the analysis criteria  then using the results that were  calculated we can compare  either the area covered shown in the  graph on the left  or the total sci-fi signal seen here on  the graph on the right  within each object mask this then allows  you to specifically quantify  the extent of mmp activity  over time  the next step once we confirm that the  assay was performing correctly and  signal was indeed observed in the  expected areas of the structure  was to add an mmp inhibitor to confirm  that cleavage of the substrate was  indeed from mmp activity  and not from being in the presence of  the components  of the major gel matrix here we used gm  6001  which is a broad spectrum mmp inhibitor  the inhibitor was added as i previously  explained  and as you can see from the images shown  here  there is a concentration dependent  decrease in the signal  from the mmp 14 substrates and  dye the signal is never completely  eliminated however as the original  tumeroid is never completely destroyed  which is similar to what has been  observed in vivo and is most likely  due to the fact that the interpreter  does not completely  penetrate the tumorite  the extent of inhibition can then be  quantified once again  using the same type of analysis  procedures that i've  that i've mentioned and confirms again  that interruption of invasion  as well as inhibition of mnp 14 activity  is indeed concentration dependent as you  can see here with a decrease  both in the area of the  of the tumeroid that you see the red  signal  as well as an actual decrease in the  signal  captured with the sci-5 imaging cube  with  increasing concentrations of the gm 6001  and shown with the decrease in the  values in the two graphs  and this can accurately also be um  be detected and measured through the  incorporation  of the citations 5 and the gen  5 data analysis software  the last step in the project was to  confirm that we were seeing  selective cleavage of the added  substrate and  subsequent signal generation from the  target mmp  here we incorporated selective  substrates  for mmp14 and mmp2  as well as the no pro substrate which is  cleaved by yeast protease  and which is not expressed in mammalian  cells  which obviously made it an ideal  negative control substrate to use in  this case  two different types of inhibitors were  tested  again gm 6001 the broad range mmp  inhibitor  and oredonin which is a selective  inhibitor  for mmp2 and mmp9  and if we look at the results first for  gm-6001  on the left here with these graphs  we see that the compound inhibits  substrate cleavage  and subsequent signal production in a  dose-dependent manner  when both mmp2 or  mmp14 substrates are incorporated and  you can see here with the decrease  again in um both in the sci-fi  signal from the um that was included in  the  the masks that were drawn around the  objects both for m  p14 here and then mmp2  then if we look at the results from  oredonin shown here on the right  again the scifi signal is inhibited in a  dose-dependent manner  when the mmp2 substrate is added  shown here again you can see at the  similar type of  shape and descending uh  values of sci-fi signal similar to what  we saw  here with gm 6001 however  when the mmp-14 substrate was  incorporated  shown here no discernible inhibition is  seen until the highest concentration  was reached which is approximately 30  micromolar  and by looking at other images that were  captured  from that well the loss in signal was  determined by us to be  uh from a site of toxic effect from the  compound  and not due to true inhibition which  agrees with published uh literature  stating that ourodonin  exhibits an apoptotic effect on mda  mb231  breast cancer cells and then finally if  we look at the results from the  no pro substrate with each compound  shown by the two graphs here  we see that only low levels of signal  are generated which would indicate that  the substrate is not being cleaved  in any appreciable manner  then a final confirmation that was  performed to  help ensure that the decrease in  sci-fi signal was not just from the fact  that the object mask was decreasing  in size upon increasing compound  concentration  was to look at the total sci-fi signal  generated  within the entire image so this is a  different type of analysis that i've  been showing  to you where we're not drawing any  object masks  around any portions of the tumeric  structure but looking at the actual  total cy5 signal that would be emanating  from the  from the entire image so closer to the  type of analysis that  you would use for a typical microplate  read using a pmt type of technology  as i mentioned this eliminates the use  of the cellular mask  and incorporates the signal from every  pixel and again  we see the same types of decrease in  signal  with both the mp14 and mmp2  substrate with increasing gm 6001  concentrations as you see here with the  with the graph on the left however the  the dose dependent decrease with the  addition of aurodonin is only seen  in the results from the mmp 2 substrate  shown  in blue and then again there's really no  appreciable change  shown with the bars in red from the mmp  14 substrate and this again confirms  that accurate results can be achieved  when combining the incense  assay components with the 3d tumor  invasion procedure  the second mechanism of action project  was performed to study the correlation  of ligand binding to the cxcr4 chemokine  receptor  as well as interruption of that binding  and then how it relates to the final  invasive phenotypic effect the  expression of  cxcr4 is tightly correlated  with the metastatic properties of breast  cancer cells  and in vivo neutralizing the interaction  of cxr  cxcr4 with its known ligand sdf1 alpha  also known as cxcl12  significantly impaired the metastasis of  breast cancer cells and cell migration  traditionally the discovery of novel  agents has been guided  by the affinity of the ligand for the  receptor under equilibrium conditions  largely ignoring the kinetic aspects  of the ligand receptor interaction  however awareness of the importance  of binding kinetics has started to  increase due to the accumulating  evidence suggesting  that the individual effectiveness of  ligands may be attributed  to the time a particular ligand resides  at its receptor or otherwise known as  the drug target resonance time  due to these findings in this project  we examine the drug target residence  time of  various cxr4 inhibitors in relation to  the known ligands  using the tablite direct homogeneous  receptor ligand binding assay that you  see here  from cis bio and in general the assay  works  in the following manner cells are  engineered to express the receptor of  interest which you see here  which contains a snap tag labeled  with a terbium molecule  a labeled ligand shown here is then  added to the well  and as binding occurs energy is  transferred from  the donor floor to the acceptor  and and then this causes the ratio of  the signal  from the acceptor compared to the donor  floor  to then increase the ratio continues to  increase as the number of binding events  also increase eventually reaching a  plateau over time which again  as previously mentioned is usually what  has been  monitored with these types of assays  then if a molecule is introduced such as  shown here in green  is uh introduced to the well which  interrupts the binding  uh this molecule preferably uh  preferentially binds to the receptor  uh kicks off the labeled ligand and  uh fret does not occur and then the  ratio  of the acceptor to the donor floor  then decreases subsequently  the final residence time value takes  into account  the observed on and off rates of the  unlabeled  inhibitors obviously and is computed by  the incorporation of the mitulski and  man equation  the on and off rates of the labeled  ligand are also necessary  components of the equation therefore  an association associative binding  experiment was first performed  using the acceptor floor labeled ligand  that i just  talked about previously then by plotting  the htrf ratios  those values over time for each of the  ligand concentrations tested which you  see  with all the different curves that have  been plotted here  the observed binding values or the k obs  can then be generated  the kd value of the ligand can also be  determined  by plotting the htrf ratios generating  after a binding equilibrium was reached  again as i showed here therefore the  the final hdf ratios would be used in  from this portion  of the curve and then plotted here  to generate the kd value that is  that is then shown we were also able to  confirm the law  of mass action by plotting the  calculated  observed association binding values or  the chaos values  that's shown here on the graph on the  right  in relation to ligand concentration  and observing a linear relationship  between the two as you can see here the  association  and uh dissociation rate constants  or the k on and the k off  using the linear regression regression  line that was calculated  can can then be generated  the resonance time of the unlabeled  inhibitor panel  was then determined in the subsequent  experiment  here the labeled ligands and unlabeled  competitors were added to the well at  the same time  and then co-incubated with the cxer for  expressing  cells during a kinetic association  experiment  from the curve fitting and the  mitalskiyan man  equation that i mentioned previously the  k on  and chaos kinetic constants of the  unlabeled competitors  were then generated as you can see here  the resonance times shown with the  values that i've circled  in red were then derived by dividing one  by the chaos value these values were  then compared to the resonance time  of the labeled ligand which you see here  from these values as well as from the  shape of the curves for the  concentrations of inhibitor tested  qualitative assumptions regarding the  constant can then be made  first if the competitor dissociates  faster  than its target from its target than the  ligand such as we see here  in the results when testing amd 3100  and wd811 the  specific binding of the ligand will  slowly  and monotonically approach its  equilibrium time and this then leads to  low resonance time such as the values  that are shown here  however when the competitor  dissociates slower such as we see with  amd 3465  and it1t the association curve of the  ligand will consist  of two distinct phases starting with a  typical overshoot such as what you see  here and here and then to decline  until a new equilibrium is released is  reached  such as we see here compounds whose  resonance  times are greater again  which we see by the residence times  generated for these two compounds  again these the values that are greater  than that of the known ligand  which was 0.44 for the labeled sdf1  alpha  may then exhibit a stronger inhibitory  response  when used in the confirmatory phenotypic  assay  which is then what we went on to perform  the inhibitor panel used in the  residence time study was then  run with the 3d tumor invasion assay  procedure such as i previously described  in the presence of a single  concentration of unlabeled  sdf1 alpha and here you can see  an example of the co-culture tumoroid  structures  again on day zero and then  after a five-day incubation in the  presence  of the chemoattractant and either amd  3465  which is shown by the images on the top  here  or in the presence of ctce 9908  such as the image you see on the bottom  whose residence time was shorter than  that of the ligand  and by the images that were captured of  the tumeric structures  in the presence of amd 3465 it's a plane  to see that the compound is exhibiting  a dose-dependent inhibition of final  tumor  tumor invasion whereas again ctce 9908  whose resonance time value was shorter  than that of the ligand exhibits  no discernible inhibitory effect on  invasion  at any of the concentrations that were  tested  the effect of rhythms time was further  confirmed by observing the results from  all six of the compounds that were  tested  it1t again whose resonance time is  longer than that of the ligand similar  to amd 3465  also inhibits invasion in a  dose-dependent manner such as what you  can see  here again similar to amd 3465  while all the other compounds that were  tested  exhibits really little or no ability to  interrupt invasion  through the 3d matrix and that's shown  by the results that you see with these  four graphs here  so in conclusion the slides and results  that i've shown today  hopefully confirm how proper  instrumentation  microplates cell models assay  chemistries and testing procedures  can provide combined processes that are  both easy to perform  provide and provide accurate  reproducible data to better understand  the target-based and phenotypic effects  of potential inhibitors of tumor  invasion and metastasis  if you'd like any additional information  specific  to all the different pro products that  were mentioned here  you can find that information by going  to  one of the websites that's listed here  specifically for each of the  of the different companies  so it looks like we have a little bit of  time to  to go and look at a few of the questions  that have  come in during the presentation so i'll  i'll do that now before we before we  finish up the first one  uh states when the um the spheroid is  first introduced  into the well do you centrifuge the  plate  and at what time and duration did you  take so  the answer is yes so it's important to  remember that  um for centrifuging the microplates  the the centrifuge should be cooled down  um  if you've ever you know dealt or had to  use  major gel obviously you'll know that  it's extremely  important to keep the the matrix cool  so that it does not begin to gel  uh you know prior to you know when it's  been  uh properly positioned uh within the  well  so the uh the centrifuge was cooled down  to  uh to four degrees celsius uh prior to  the centrifugation process  and then um the  for centrifugation i  usually would uh would spin the plates  for  uh just a matter of um a minute or two  uh typically at about 200 times  times g again in a swing buckets  rotor type of type of a centrifuge this  obviously  can be optimized depending on the type  of centrifuge that you have  in your lab and uh you know also  depending on the type of  cell models that you are that you're  using  okay the second is do you have a list of  cell lines  uh responsive to cxel12 in this assay  other than mda mb231  um i i do not uh what we did was this  this project was run  as a example or a confirmatory type of a  project to show  that this type of assay could be  performed  you know in co